<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rammelsberg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rammelsberg is a mountain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> near Goslar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and lead ores were mined for over 1,000 years, from at least the 10th century until 1988. Its wealth drew the Salian emperors to build their palace at Goslar and made the town prosperous. The mine is now a museum and, with Goslar's old town, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rammelsberg.jpg" alt="Rammelsberg" width="320" height="135" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Axel Hindemith · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rammelsberg_Bergwerksanlagen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Rammelsberg rises to 635 metres on the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>, south of Goslar. Unlike the ore of the Upper Harz, its deposits were laid down in the Devonian period by hot, metal-bearing springs on the sea floor, forming two large lenses of massive sulfide that were later folded and overturned within the mountain. The Old Bed was exposed at the surface by erosion; the New Bed was found by deliberate exploration only in the 19th century. The ore held on average 14% zinc, 6% lead, 2% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, 1 g/t gold and 140 g/t <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><p>Legend derives the name from Ramm, a knight of Emperor Otto the Great, whose horse pawed the ground while he hunted in 968 and laid bare a vein of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Other explanations point to the ramsons growing on the slopes or, most probably, to an old word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a> records around 968 that Otto the Great had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> veins opened, and the mining settlement of Goslar is first named in 979. The mines were imperial property. In 1175 the Welf duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> demanded them as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> in return for aiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombard-league">Lombard League</a>; refused, he besieged Goslar and wrecked the workings, which were restored after his fall in 1180. Goslar's council later held the mines in pledge and bought the mining rights in 1359. In 1376 an accident killed more than 100 miners.</p><p>In 1552 Duke Henry V of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> seized the mines from the weakened city after the Schmalkaldic War, and his son Duke Julius promoted them from 1568. With Goslar the Rammelsberg passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in 1814 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866. Under the Rammelsbergprojekt of 1936/37 the Nazi authorities had the mine greatly expanded as part of the Four-year plan, once froth flotation had solved the problem of dressing its ore, with new surface buildings by Fritz Schupp and Martin Kremmer. Preussag closed the exhausted mine on 30 June 1988, after almost 30 million tonnes of ore had been raised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A citizens' association opposed plans to demolish the surface buildings, and the mine became a museum and visitor mine. With Goslar's Old Town it was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1992, extended in 2010 to the Upper Harz Water Regale, Walkenried Abbey and the Samson Pit. Its survivals include the Roeder Gallery with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">water wheels</a>, the Rathstiefste drainage gallery and the Master Malter's Tower of around 1500.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rammelsberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The discovery of silver in the <b>Rammelsberg</b> near Goslar, traditionally dated to around 968, gave the kings a rich source of coinage, and Goslar became a favoured royal residence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Silver was found in the <b>Rammelsberg</b> near Goslar in the 10th century, and the Salian emperors built an imperial palace there, where Henry III often resided.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-goslar">Free Imperial City of Goslar</a></li></ul></section>
